# Deploying Guideline (Docker Compose + Nginx)

This guideline is a reusable production deployment flow for web projects using:
- Docker images in a registry
- Docker Compose on a Linux server
- Nginx as reverse proxy

---

## 1) Prerequisites

- A Linux server with public IP
- Docker and Docker Compose plugin installed
- Domain DNS control (A record)
- Docker registry account (Docker Hub or private registry)
- Project has:
  - `docker-compose.yaml`
  - production `.env` template/variables
  - Nginx vhost config (or you create one)

Check:
- `docker --version`
- `docker compose version`
- `nginx -v`

---

## 2) Prepare Project for Deploy

1. Ensure compose uses image tags (avoid local build on server if not needed):
   - `image: your-registry/your-app:TAG`
2. Ensure required env vars are defined in root `.env`.
3. Ensure service ports are correct and not conflicting.
4. If compose declares external network, create it first:
   - `docker network create <network_name> || true`

---

## 3) Build and Push Images (from local/CI)

Example:
- `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile.backend -t <registry>/<backend-image>:<tag> .`
- `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile.frontend -t <registry>/<frontend-image>:<tag> .`
- `docker push <registry>/<backend-image>:<tag>`
- `docker push <registry>/<frontend-image>:<tag>`

Best practice:
- Keep one immutable tag per release (`v1.0.1`, `2026-04-27-1`)
- Avoid reusing the same tag in production when possible

---

## 4) Server Setup

1. SSH into server:
   - `ssh <user>@<server-ip>`
2. Create deploy folder and upload project files:
   - `mkdir -p ~/apps/<project-name>`
3. Place files:
   - `docker-compose.yaml`
   - `.env`
   - Nginx site config

If Docker Hub rate limit/private repo:
- `docker login`

---

## 5) Start Services with Docker Compose

From project folder:
- `docker network create <network_name> || true` (if needed)
- `docker compose pull`
- `docker compose up -d`
- `docker compose ps`

Health checks:
- `docker compose logs <service> --tail=200`
- `docker compose logs -f <backend-service>`

---

## 6) Configure Nginx Reverse Proxy

1. Copy vhost:
   - `sudo cp ./<site>.conf /etc/nginx/sites-available/<site>.conf`
2. Enable site:
   - `sudo ln -sf /etc/nginx/sites-available/<site>.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/<site>.conf`
3. Test + reload:
   - `sudo nginx -t`
   - `sudo systemctl reload nginx`

Recommended proxy pattern:
- `location /api/` -> backend upstream
- `location /` -> frontend upstream
- Upstream names in `proxy_pass` must exactly match `upstream` block names

---

## 7) DNS and Firewall

1. DNS A records:
   - `your-domain.com -> <server-ip>`
   - `www.your-domain.com -> <server-ip>` (optional)
2. Open firewall ports:
   - `80/tcp` (required)
   - `443/tcp` (if HTTPS)
   - app ports only if direct debug needed

Verify:
- `dig +short your-domain.com`
- `curl -I http://your-domain.com`

---

## 8) Verify End-to-End

On server:
- `curl -I http://127.0.0.1:<frontend-port>`
- `curl -I http://127.0.0.1:<backend-port>`
- `curl -I http://127.0.0.1` (through nginx)
- `docker compose ps`
- `sudo nginx -t`

Expected:
- backend healthy
- frontend up
- nginx test successful

---

## 9) Common Issues and Fixes

### A) `service "db-init" didn't complete successfully: exit 1`
Likely DB auth mismatch or stale DB volume.
- Check logs:
  - `docker compose logs db-init --tail=200`
  - `docker compose logs mysql --tail=200`
- If safe to reset data:
  - `docker compose down -v`
  - `docker compose up -d`

### B) `network <name> declared as external, but could not be found`
- `docker network create <name>`

### C) Nginx `host not found in upstream ...`
- `proxy_pass` target does not match `upstream` block name
- Fix names, then:
  - `sudo nginx -t`
  - `sudo systemctl reload nginx`

### D) `sudo: unable to resolve host <hostname>`
- Fix `/etc/hosts` to include hostname mapping:
  - `127.0.1.1 <hostname>`

### E) Backend unhealthy in compose
- Check backend logs:
  - `docker logs <backend-container> --tail=300`
- Typical reasons:
  - DB not reachable
  - wrong `DATABASE_URL` or secrets
  - startup tasks (migrations/seed) blocking boot too long

---

## 10) Release Update Workflow

For each new release:
1. Build and push new image tags
2. Update `IMAGE_TAG` (or image tag) on server
3. Run:
   - `docker compose pull`
   - `docker compose up -d`
4. Validate:
   - `docker compose ps`
   - health/log checks
   - external URL smoke test

---

## 11) Optional: Full Docker Cleanup (Use Carefully)

This removes all containers/images/volumes/build cache on the machine:
- `docker ps -aq | xargs -r docker rm -f`
- `docker system prune -a --volumes -f`
- `docker builder prune -a -f`

Warning:
- This is destructive and impacts all projects on that server.

---

## 12) Move Local Data To Server (DB + Public)

Use when you want production to start with your current local data.

1. On local machine (Windows):
   - `./package-local-data.ps1`
   - This creates `deploy/package` with:
     - `db.sql` (full MySQL dump of `vuleits_db`)
     - `seed.db.snapshot.json`
     - `public/frontend` and `public/backend`

2. Upload `deploy/package` to server project folder.

3. On server after deploy:
   - `bash deploy/restore-server-data.sh`
   - This imports DB dump and syncs public folders.

4. Restart app containers:
   - `docker compose -f docker-compose.yaml up -d`

